// Practice quiz: Method references
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: collections-streams/lambdas-and-streams/method-references

::o1 static::A lambda receives text and returns Integer.parseInt(text). Which method reference is equivalent? {
=Integer\:\:parseInt#Correct. parseInt is static, so the reference names its class, Integer.
~text\:\:parseInt#Not quite. parseInt is a static method of Integer, not a method of the String text.
~Integer.parseInt()#Not quite. That is a method call with no argument, not a reference.
~String\:\:parseInt#Not quite. String has no parseInt method; it belongs to Integer.
}

::o1 bound::log is a List of String. A lambda receives line and calls log.add(line). Which method reference is equivalent? {
=log\:\:add#Correct. The reference is bound to the one list log, and line becomes the argument to add.
~List\:\:add#Not quite. That is unbound\: it expects the list itself as an extra first argument.
~log.add#Not quite. Without \:\: this is not a method reference.
~add\:\:log#Not quite. The receiver comes before \:\: and the method name after it.
}

::o1 unbound receiver::String\:\:toUpperCase is used as a Function from String to String. Which String does toUpperCase run on? {
=The String passed to the function when it is applied#Correct. An unbound reference uses its first argument as the receiver.
~One fixed String chosen when the reference is created#Not quite. That describes a bound reference such as name\:\:toUpperCase.
~The String class itself, as a static call#Not quite. toUpperCase is an instance method, so it needs a String receiver.
~A new empty String#Not quite. The receiver is the argument, not a new String.
}

::o1 two-argument unbound::A Comparator of String is written as a lambda that receives a and b and returns a.compareTo(b). Which method reference is equivalent? {
=String\:\:compareTo#Correct. The first argument a becomes the receiver and b becomes the argument.
~a\:\:compareTo#Not quite. a is a lambda parameter and does not exist where the reference is written.
~String\:\:compare#Not quite. String has no compare method; the lambda calls compareTo.
~Comparator\:\:compareTo#Not quite. compareTo is a String method, so the type before \:\: is String.
}

::o1 constructor::Which method reference can be a Supplier that makes a new, empty ArrayList on every call? {
=ArrayList\:\:new#Correct. Type\:\:new refers to a constructor, and the no-argument one fits a Supplier.
~new ArrayList()#Not quite. That makes one list right away; it is not a Supplier.
~List\:\:new#Not quite. List is an interface and has no constructor.
~ArrayList\:\:create#Not quite. ArrayList has no create method; constructors are referred to with new.
}

::o2 chained calls::A lambda receives s and returns s.trim().length(). Why can it not become one method reference? {
=It calls two methods in a chain, and a method reference forwards to exactly one#Correct. Keep the lambda, or name a method that does both steps.
~trim is a static method#Not quite. trim is an instance method of String.
~It returns an int#Not quite. An int result is fine; String\:\:length also returns one.
~It can, as String\:\:trim\:\:length#Not quite. That is not valid Java; \:\: names one method only.
}

::o2 no existing method::A lambda receives n and returns n * 2. Can it be replaced by a method reference? {
=No, because it does arithmetic instead of forwarding n to an existing method#Correct. A method reference needs one method or constructor that already does the work.
~Yes, as Integer\:\:multiply#Not quite. Integer has no multiply method.
~Yes, as Math\:\:multiplyExact#Not quite. multiplyExact takes two arguments, but this lambda receives only n.
~Yes, as n\:\:times#Not quite. n is a lambda parameter, and int has no methods.
}
